  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "What is the missing digit in the following equality?\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{7}÷\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{\\,\\boxed{}}{7}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Dividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\frac{2}{5}$ is $\\frac{5}{2}.$ So we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{7}÷\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{4}{7}\\times \\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{7}\\times \\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{4\\times 5}{7\\times 2}=\\frac{20}{14}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\frac{20}{14}=\\frac{20÷2}{14÷2}=\\frac{10}{7}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $10.$",
